{
    "title": "Expiration of Interim Final Rule: Control of Communicable Diseases; Foreign Quarantine",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 04, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-04/pdf/2025-21962.pdf",
    "summary": "Today\u2019s Federal Register features three quiet but meaningful updates: the Coast Guard set a new security buffer around its base in San Juan, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention ended a COVID\u2011era airline data rule, and federal fisheries managers shifted small amounts of fish quota between states for the end of the year.\n\nFor travelers and airlines, the biggest change is that the CDC has let its pandemic rule requiring airlines to collect and share certain passenger and crew information expire. The health emergency ended in 2023, and the agency now says there\u2019s no longer a need for that temporary tool. Passengers and carriers may welcome fewer data requests and smoother operations. On the other hand, public health officials and some advocates may worry that it could be harder to quickly reach travelers if a future outbreak emerges and new tools aren\u2019t already in place.\n\nIn Puerto Rico, the Coast Guard created a 200\u2011foot security zone around the water\u2019s edge of Base San Juan. The goal is straightforward: keep people and vessels at a safe distance to protect the base and those who work there. Security and emergency responders benefit from clearer boundaries and quicker enforcement. Local boaters, anglers, and waterfront users may feel the downside through reduced access and the need to request permission before entering the area. The agency says this change doesn\u2019t add new paperwork, doesn\u2019t impose major costs on governments or businesses, and falls within existing environmental and federal\u2011tribal guidelines.\n\nAlong the East Coast, year\u2011end adjustments moved a small share of commercial catch limits between states. North Carolina sent a modest amount of summer flounder quota to Massachusetts to account for a landing made under a safety\u2011related \u201csafe harbor\u201d stop earlier in the season. Rhode Island transferred some bluefish quota to North Carolina. These shifts help fishermen and dealers in the receiving states settle up and stay compliant. Donor states give up a bit of potential harvest, though typically by agreement when they have room to share. In Massachusetts, federal landings for summer flounder had already been closed in November, so the change mainly updates the books rather than reopening fishing.\n\nTaken together, these actions tidy up pandemic\u2011era policies, sharpen a local security boundary, and balance end\u2011of\u2011year fishing limits. None create major new costs, but each has practical effects: less data collection for travelers, more restricted waters near a Coast Guard base, and small gains or givebacks for fishing communities as the year closes.",
